  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   We propose a multi-resolution Bayesian optimization approach to find a step function that minimizes the maximum overlap integral. This method addresses the limitations of previous optimization techniques by combining global search with local refinement and resolution scaling. The approach maintains a probabilistic distribution over candidate solutions and uses acquisition functions to balance exploitation and exploration. This strategy allows escaping local optima more effectively than the gradient-based methods used previously. The method is particularly suited for the continuous nature of the problem and provides a mathematical framework for comparison.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   We represent the function h as a piecewise-constant function with n samples over [0,2]. The objective is to minimize:\n   max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx \n   subject to:\n   - 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n   - \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n\n   We use a Gaussian process regression model to approximate the objective function and its constraints. The input space is the function values h, transformed to [min_val, max_val] using bounds. The output is the maximum overlap integral. We maintain both continuous and binary representations for diversity.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Data structures:\n      - GaussianProcessModel: Tracks the objective surface\n      - BayesianOptimization: Class for managing the optimization process\n      - FunctionRepresentation: Base class for function representations\n\n   b. Core implementation steps:\n      1. Parameter initialization:\n         - Base resolution: 512 samples\n         - Initial samples: 20 random feasible points (using the project function)\n         - Number of iterations: 500\n\n      2. Optimization loop:\n         - Gradient estimation requires:\n             - Objective function with shape (n,)\n             - Constraint function: return (h>=0)&(h<=1)\n             - Use finite-differences for gradient estimation\n         - Gaussian process regression\n         - Expected improvement acquisition function\n         - New sample selection using Thompson sampling\n\n      3. Multi-resolution strategy:\n         - Main representation: 512 samples\n         - When a candidate achieves low C5 value, promote it to:\n             * Binary representation (896 samples)\n             * Continuous representation (1024 samples)\n         - Use resolution mapping: interpolate between sample values\n\n      4. Crossover operator (for multi-resolution):\n         - Choose two parents from highest and lowest representations\n         - Create children by:\n             * Copying sections from parents\n             * Adding Gaussian noise\n             * Maintaining integral constraint through projection\n\n      5. Evaluation and selection: \n         - Compute C5 using the full correlation method\n         - Fitness = 1/C5 (lower is better)\n         - Selection based on expected improvement\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   a. Default configurations:\n      - Base resolution: 512\n      - Iterations: 500\n      - Bayesian loop: 20 phases\n      - Hyperparameters:\n          * GP kernel: Mat\u00e9rn 5/2\n          * Acquisition: Expected Improvement\n          * Noise variance: Optimized per objective\n\n   b. Variations to try:\n      1. Resolution scaling: Binary then continuous (896->1024)\n      2. Reparameterization: Use transformed variables with Gaussian priors\n      3. Acquisition functions: IBond, Knowledge Gradient\n      4. Gaussian process kernels: Radial basis, Mat\u00e9rn, Automatic Relevance\n\n   c. Trigger conditions:\n      - Detect plateau (5 consecutive evaluations without improvement)\n      - Automatically try different kernels based on improvement rate\n      - Adjust GP parameters based on training error\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   a. Time estimation:\n      - Base resolution: 512 samples\n      - GP training: O(m^3) where m is sample size\n      - Typical evaluation: ~1-2 seconds/phase\n      - Total budget: ~20 phases * 55 seconds = ~1100 seconds\n\n   b. Termination checklist:\n      - Check for NaN/Inf in C5 values\n      - Minimum improvement threshold (1e-6)\n      - Time threshold (1050 seconds)\n\n   c. Fallback strategy:\n      - Primary: Late GP optimization (final 10 iterations)\n      - Fallback: Use initial population or provided initial_h_values\n      - Recovery: Combine best individuals with new random samples\n\n   d. Resource management:\n      - Monitor memory usage for storage (samples, kernels)\n      - Use least recently used caching for function evaluations\n\n6. Validation and contract\n   a. Feasibility checks:\n      - np.all((h>=0)&(h<=1))\n      - np.abs(sum(h)-n/2.0) < 5.0   (project function parameter)\n      - np.array([f(x) for x in C5_condition])\n\n   b. Scoring:\n      - Primary score: C5 value\n      - Secondary score: Resolution\n      - Final evaluation: Compute C5 on final solution\n\n   c. Checks:\n      - Optimizer should maintain candidate history\n      - Gaussian process should be updated at each step\n      - Solve should transform the function back to analytic form if needed (though not required by problem)\n\nImplementation note: All numerical work uses numpy/scipy. The GaussianProcessRegressor from sklearn is recommended. The GP model should handle noise based on function evaluations and have early termination capabilities.",
